# Vulnerability details

## Impact
In the `AuraVault::mint`, `AuraVault::deposit` and `AuraVault::claim` functions, there is an approval of assets to the rewardPool. However, the safeApprove function of Openzeppelin doesn't allow an approval of non zero amount if the spender already been approved non zero amount from the owner.
The above functions don't check for the approve 0 before approving a non zero amount, which leads to revert of the transaction, causing a Denial of Service.

## Proof of Concept

-`deposit` function
```solidity
      function deposit(uint256 assets, address receiver) public virtual override(IERC4626, ERC4626) returns (uint256) {
        uint256 shares = previewDeposit(assets);
        _deposit(_msgSender(), receiver, assets, shares);

        // Deposit  in reward pool
@>      IERC20(asset()).safeApprove(rewardPool, assets);
        IPool(rewardPool).deposit(assets, address(this));

        return shares;
    }
```
-`mint` function
```solidity
       function mint(uint256 shares, address receiver) public virtual override(IERC4626, ERC4626) returns (uint256) {
        uint256 assets = previewMint(shares);
        _deposit(_msgSender(), receiver, assets, shares);

        // Deposit assets in reward pool
@>      IERC20(asset()).safeApprove(rewardPool, assets);
        IPool(rewardPool).deposit(assets, address(this));

        return assets;
    }
```
-`claim` function:
```solidity

    function claim(uint256[] memory amounts, uint256 maxAmountIn) external returns (uint256 amountIn) {

         // ...

        // Compound assets into "asset" balance
@>      IERC20(asset()).safeApprove(rewardPool, amountIn);
        IPool(rewardPool).deposit(amountIn, address(this));

        // ...
    }

```

## Tools Used
Manual analysis.

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
`safeApprove` should only be called when setting an initial allowance, or when resetting it to zero. To increase and decrease it, use `safeIncreaseAllowance` and `safeDecreaseAllowance`





