# Vulnerability details

## Impact
Chainlink oracle will return the wrong price for asset if the underlying aggregator hits minAnswer. In the event that an asset crashes (i.e. LUNA) the protocol can be manipulated to give out loans at an inflated price because there is no check for minAnswer from the aggregator. 

## Vulnerability details
Note: This issue is different from [analyzer M-4](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-07-loopfi/blob/main/4naly3er-report.md#m-4-chainlinks-latestrounddata-might-return-stale-or-incorrect-results) as it is caused by a lack of check for minPrice/maxPrice from the agregator. Examples of issues related to `analyzer M-4 ` are these issue: [1](https://github.com/code-423n4/2022-12-tigris-findings/issues/655), [2](https://github.com/code-423n4/2022-10-inverse-findings/issues/584) and [3](https://github.com/sherlock-audit/2023-02-blueberry-judging/issues/94)

Chainlink aggregators have a built in circuit breaker if the price of an asset goes outside of a predetermined price band. The result is that if an asset experiences a huge drop in value (i.e. LUNA crash) the price of the oracle will continue to return the minPrice instead of the actual price of the asset. This would allow user to continue borrowing with the asset but at the wrong price. This is exactly what happened to [Venus on BSC when LUNA imploded](https://rekt.news/venus-blizz-rekt/).
## Proof of Concept
As seen below, there is no check for minPrice/maxPrice from the aggregator. [ChainlinkOracle.sol#L106](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-07-loopfi/blob/57871f64bdea450c1f04c9a53dc1a78223719164/src/oracle/ChainlinkOracle.sol#L106)
```solidity
    function _fetchAndValidate(address token) internal view returns (bool isValid, uint256 price) {
        Oracle memory oracle = oracles[token];
        try AggregatorV3Interface(oracle.aggregator).latestRoundData() returns (
            uint80 /*roundId*/,
            int256 answer,
            uint256 /*startedAt*/,
            uint256 updatedAt,
            uint80 /*answeredInRound*/
        ) {
            isValid = (answer > 0 && block.timestamp - updatedAt <= oracle.stalePeriod);
@>          return (isValid, wdiv(uint256(answer), oracle.aggregatorScale));
        } catch {
            // return the default values (false, 0) on failure
        }

    }
```

There is also no check for minPrice/maxPrice from the aggregator in the `PendleLPOracle`. [PendleLPOracle.sol#L121](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-07-loopfi/blob/57871f64bdea450c1f04c9a53dc1a78223719164/src/oracle/PendleLPOracle.sol#L121)

```solidity
    function _fetchAndValidate() internal view returns (bool isValid, uint256 price) {
        try AggregatorV3Interface(aggregator).latestRoundData() returns (
            uint80 roundId, int256 answer, uint256 /*startedAt*/, uint256 updatedAt, uint80 answeredInRound
        ) {
            isValid = (answer > 0 && answeredInRound >= roundId && block.timestamp - updatedAt <= stalePeriod);
@>          return (isValid, wdiv(uint256(answer), aggregatorScale));
        } catch {
            // return the default values (false, 0) on failure
        }
    }
```

Chainlink's latestRoundData pulls the associated aggregator and requests round data from it. Chainlink Aggregators have minPrice and maxPrice circuit breakers built into them. This means that if the price of the asset drops below the minPrice, the protocol will continue to value the token at minPrice instead of it's actual value. This will allow users to take out huge amounts of bad debt and bankrupt the protocol.

## Tools Used
Manual Review, Previous findings: [#18](https://github.com/sherlock-audit/2023-02-blueberry-judging/issues/18)

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
Check the returned answer against the minPrice/maxPrice and revert if the answer is outside of the bounds.
In `ChainlinkOracle`:

```solidity
    function _fetchAndValidate(address token) internal view returns (bool isValid, uint256 price) {
        Oracle memory oracle = oracles[token];
        try AggregatorV3Interface(oracle.aggregator).latestRoundData() returns (
            uint80 /*roundId*/,
            int256 answer,
            uint256 /*startedAt*/,
            uint256 updatedAt,
            uint80 /*answeredInRound*/
        ) {
            isValid = (answer > 0 && block.timestamp - updatedAt <= oracle.stalePeriod);
            return (isValid, wdiv(uint256(answer), oracle.aggregatorScale));
+           if (answer >= maxPrice or answer <= minPrice) revert();
        } catch {
            // return the default values (false, 0) on failure
        }
    }
```

In `PendleLPOracle`:
```solidity
    function _fetchAndValidate() internal view returns (bool isValid, uint256 price) {
        try AggregatorV3Interface(aggregator).latestRoundData() returns (
            uint80 roundId, int256 answer, uint256 /*startedAt*/, uint256 updatedAt, uint80 answeredInRound
        ) {
            isValid = (answer > 0 && answeredInRound >= roundId && block.timestamp - updatedAt <= stalePeriod);
+           if (answer >= maxPrice or answer <= minPrice) revert();
            return (isValid, wdiv(uint256(answer), aggregatorScale));
        } catch {
            // return the default values (false, 0) on failure
        }
    }
```





